 ## tab-keys.mjs
 
-### §stable-tab-keys — closing a tab never renumbers the others
-- Since: 2026-09-02
-- Decision: a key is assigned once from `next` and is never reused or shifted. Closing a tab leaves a gap (`t1,t3`).
-- Forbidden: renumbering the remaining keys after a close to make them contiguous.
-- Reason: the key is the React key and the URL state. Shifting keys remounts every tab to the right of the closed one
-  and drops its scroll position and unsaved form input (incident 2026-08-29, reproduced by the "close keeps keys" test).
-- Evidence: `tab-keys.test.mjs`
+### §contiguous-tab-keys — closing a tab renumbers the others (formerly §stable-tab-keys)
+- Since: 2026-10-22 — the maintainer overrode §stable-tab-keys (2026-09-02).
+- Decision: `closeTab` renumbers the remaining tabs in order so keys are always contiguous (`t1,t2,…,tn`), and resets
+  `next` to n+1 so the next open continues the sequence.
+- Forbidden: leaving gaps in the keys after a close (`t1,t3`).
+- Reason: the maintainer chose contiguous keys over stable ones. Superseded decision: keys were assigned once and
+  never shifted, because the key is the React key and the URL state, and shifting remounts every tab to the right of
+  the closed one, dropping its scroll position and unsaved form input (incident 2026-08-29). That cost now applies
+  wherever the key is used as the React key.
+- Evidence: `tab-keys.test.mjs` ("close renumbers keys", "open after close continues contiguously")
